Z||000000||kinboyatuwo||11-13-2001||07:11 PM||||Ok, i am now sure there is movement in the rear end. The drainage hole under the driverside has rusted away to the spot where the cradle mount is attached to the bottom of the car. I am sure thee is movement because i did this in a parking lot<BR>Put my finger where the crack is <BR>accelerated and felt about 2-3mm of movement<BR>it moved back  and forth as i accelerated, this is the looseness in the rear end when accelerating and cornering<P>attached is a basic scetch (not too good i am new with photo shop) <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/kinboyatuwo/Car1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/kinboyatuwo/Car1.html</A> <P>Now the question, how to fix it?<P>I think welding a brace from the part where the mount attaches to a spot forward where is is in good condiion and clean and seal it up<P>any oppinions or ideas, options?<P>thanks, help asap would be good<P>kin<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/b4lower50.jpg"> <BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||11-13-2001||07:34 PM||||That's not good...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><BR>You can slap in a cross-member. My Dad's Alfa had that done when the unibody fell apart... It's fine, stiffer than before... But the underside is FUGLY with a a couple of I-beams tacked in... It's your call. <P>For more work, but nicer results, you could always find a nice, blown-motor/tranny Fiero, and start switching parts. Use the frame only  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||9
Z||000002||Old Lar||11-13-2001||08:49 PM||||Rust, once started is unstoppable.  Frame rust is bad news, and makes the car very unsafe to drive.  It may split apart at any time.  Welding in braces will shore the car up for a while, but it is time to start looking for a replacement and use your current car as a parts doner.<P>Sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||kinboyatuwo||11-13-2001||10:29 PM||||Well that is what i was thinking, i will put in an i beam and look for a new gt in the spring, was going to look any ways.<BR>and you can stop rust, its called ashphalt tar, always works.<BR>Any other opinions or help would be good<P>kin<P>(looks like tomorrow is the day to do it)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/b4lower50.jpg"> <BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||4
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||11-13-2001||10:54 PM||||Rust is terrable on cars in the north. You don't even want to know how rusted my 84se is. I basicly have the thing riged up right now, hoping it will take me through the winter. I am planing to part out my car in the spring because the frame is geting pretty bad. Be carefull with it rusted, you dont want something to snap while on the highway doing 140 clicks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>I think ill fly down to florida or somewhere down south for my next used car   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">.<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||11-13-2001||10:58 PM||||Oh ya about stoping rust.<BR>On rusted parts of the car allready sand down as much as you can. Then spray on a "rust converter" paint, this stuff turns the rust you didn't/couldn't sand away black/silver and stops anymore from forming. After that spray on a tar undercoating. For parts not rusted allready just clean them and spray on the undercoating or paint them with an anti-rust spray paint.<P>This worked great for me! Good luck!||||reg||14
